ion and tachycardia", "Acute Gastroenteritis: Considered due to the recent contact with a person with similar symptoms, but the severity of hypertension and tachycardia suggests a more complex underlying issue", "Acute Coronary Syndrome: Considered due to the systolic murmur and severe hypertension, but the primary presenting syndrome is gastrointestinal"], "alarm_features": ["Severe hypertension (197/128 mmHg)", "Tachycardia (100/min)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Acute Gastrointestinal Infection", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2, "posterior": 0.2763978755375559, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["The recent contact with a person with vomiting and diarrhea supports the diagnosis of Acute Gastrointestinal Infection."], "evidence_against": ["Severe hypertension and tachycardia argue against Acute Gastrointestinal Infection."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Acute Abdominal Catastrophe", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.41459681330633386, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Severe hypertension, tachycardia, and weak pulses in the lower extremities support the diagnosis of Acute Abdominal Catastrophe."], "evidence_against": ["The absence of significant abdominal pain or peritoneal signs argues against Acute Abdominal Catastrophe."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3", "B2.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "severity", "representative_diseases": [