t": 3, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B4", "B2.1", "B2.2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=71%, source=both]\n\nAcute Gastrointestinal Infection vs Acute Mesenteric Ischemia:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal cortical gyration\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal isoelectric focusing of serum transferrin, abnormal skull morphology\n\nAcute Gastrointestinal Infection vs Acute Bowel Obstruction:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abdominal cramping, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abdominal swelling\n\nAcute Gastrointestinal Infection vs Acute Diverticulitis:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cortical gyration\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abdominal tenderness, abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal ekg\n\nAcute Gastrointestinal Infection vs Acute Pancreatitis:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, autosomal dominant inheritance, autosomal recessive inheritance, chronic pancreatitis, fatty stool\n\nAcute Mesenteric Ischemia vs Acute Bowel Obstruction:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abdominal cramping, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abdominal swelling\n\nAcute Mesenteric Ischemia vs Acute Diverticulitis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abdominal tenderness, abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nAcute Mesenteric Ischemia vs Acute Pancreatitis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, autosomal dominant inheritance, chronic pancreatitis, fatty stool, fever\n\nAcute Bowel Obstruction vs Acute Diverticulitis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, abdominal cramping, abdominal distention, abdominal swelling, blood in stool\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n\nAcute Bowel Obstruction vs Acute Pancreatitis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, abdominal cramping, abdominal distention, abdominal swelling, abdominal tenderness\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, autosomal recessive inheritance, chronic pancreatitis, fatty stool, insulin-dependent but ketosis-resistant diabetes\n\nAcute Diverticulitis vs Acute Pancreatitis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal tenderness, abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, autosomal recessive inheritance, chronic pancreatitis, fatty stool, insulin-dependent but ketosis-resistant diabetes\n\nRelated to Acute Gastrointestinal Infection: otitis externa\n\nRelated to Acute Mesenteric Ischemia: ischemic bowel disease\n\nRelated to Acute Diverticulitis: diverticulitis of colon, inflammatory disease, intestinal disease\n\nRelated to Acute Pancreatitis: acute disease, alcohol-related disorders, autoimmune pancreatitis\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week → feeding difficulties → Acute Gastrointestinal Infection\n  A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week → vomiting → Acute Diverticulitis\n  A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week → nausea → Acute Diverticulitis"}
